Subject: [PATCH 11/66] x86: signed 32-bit immediate shifts extend only on wide
 demand

The immediate-shift lowering appended `cltq` after every signed 32-bit
`shll`/`sarl`.  It predates the value-level demand analysis
(signed_i32_home_requirements) that the neighbouring imul path already
consults: a 32-bit shift zero-fills the upper half, and only a consumer
that reads the 8-byte home raw (address arithmetic, 64-bit
binops/compares/selects/stores/arguments, the x87 F128 cast) needs the
canonical extension.  Explicit widening casts extend on their own, so
the old code stacked `sarl; cltq; movslq` (the byte-sum vector end).

Uses emit_sext32_for_value (the needs_sext superset, which keeps the x87
source conservative), exactly as the imul path.

