From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:48:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/66] x86 peephole: fold a scaled index into an
 absolute-symbol memory operand

Non-PIC table indexing reaches the peephole as `shlq $k, %R; OP sym+d(%R)`
(PIC stages the symbol in a register and already takes the `addq` arm of
fold_shift_into_sib). Rewrite the consumer to `OP sym+d(, %R, 2^k)` and
delete the shift: both address d + (R << k) and the displacement keeps its
R_X86_64_32S relocation, matching GCC 16.2's `addq $1, bins(,%r11,8)`.

Obligations (shift_into_mem_index): 64-bit non-%rsp base, the only mention
of %R in the consumer, no SIB index / implicit use / register in the
displacement (no %rip, no segment), the scaled value dead after the
consumer, the consumer reads no flag (adc/sbb/cmov would see shl's CF),
and shl's flags are either fully rewritten by the consumer or dead.

Measured (Callgrind Ir, -fno-pie vs PIE, full 74-program A/B): histogram
went from +7.85% worse than PIE to -2.61% better; PIC asm is byte-identical
on all 74 programs (the arm never fires under PIC). Gate contract 2c in
check_rmw_sib_folds.sh pins the fold at -fno-pie and fails on the parent.
