From 03a5eea7312da3d7562bec8241e8e80f3c23537c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:54:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/66] driver: GCC option cancellation and level-scoped x86
 ISA denials

Pre-existing driver bugs, measured against gcc-16 -m32 -dM -E on 28 flag
combinations (all now match):
 * a later -mX / -mno-X deletes every earlier occurrence of the same option
   together with what it implied (GCC `cancel_option`): `-mno-sse3 -mavx`
   is SSE2..AVX, `-mavx2 -mno-sse2 -msse2` keeps AVX2. lccc applied flags
   cumulatively, so an early denial wrongly survived a later grant;
 * a denial clears its own level and above only: `-mssse3 -mno-sse4.1`
   keeps SSSE3, `-msse3 -mno-ssse3` keeps SSE3. lccc over-denied.

The classifier deciding "is this an x86 ISA flag" applies the flag to a
scratch Driver (pure), so it can never disagree with the real parser.
`-mred-zone` (GCC's x86-64 default) is accepted: it only permits a red
zone lccc never uses, and refusing it broke `-mno-red-zone -mred-zone`.

The regression test asserts the predefined macros (the layer GCC was
measured on) through a real Preprocessor, plus the resolved SSE3 level;
since #972 `effective_x86_features` reports ISA permissions, kept apart
from the i686 vectorizer ceiling.

#972's i686_native_avx_permissions_keep_vectorizer_ceiling_closed expected
`-m32 -mavx2 -mno-avx2` to keep AVX. GCC 16.2 predefines only __SSE2__
for it (-m32 and -m64 alike): the later -mno-avx2 cancels -mavx2 together
with its implied AVX. The row now expects no AVX, citing the oracle.
