Subject: [PATCH 27/66] x86: fence x87 out under
 -mno-80387/-msoft-float/-mgeneral-regs-only
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pre-existing: `no_x87` was parsed but never read (#[allow(dead_code)]),
so -mno-80387, -msoft-float and -mgeneral-regs-only still produced x87
code on i686 and for long double on x86-64 — silently breaking the "no
FPU registers" contract kernel objects rely on.

GCC 16.2, measured: MASK_80387 is one last-wins bit cleared by
-mno-80387 / -msoft-float / -mgeneral-regs-only and set by -m80387 /
-mhard-float (`-mgeneral-regs-only -m80387` = x87, `-m80387
-mgeneral-regs-only` = none); a later -msse2 does NOT revive scalar FP on
-m32 (fpmath=387). Without x87 GCC -m32 emits soft-float libcalls
(__adddf3, __fixdfsi, ...) that stock i686 libgcc does not even provide
(only compiler-rt does); -m64 refuses x87 returns. lccc has no soft-float
lowering, so — by explicit decision — it fails closed: the final text is
fenced for any x87 instruction (exact vocabulary: every x87 mnemonic
starts with `f`; FXSAVE/FXRSTOR/FEMMS excluded), naming function and
instruction. Every x87 instruction lccc emits carries an FP value (64-bit
atomics use cmpxchg8b), so integer-only units compile exactly as before.

-mno-fp-ret-in-387 is NOT "no x87" (GCC keeps x87 arithmetic and returns
float/double in %eax / %edx:%eax): it is now its own flag, and every
emitted definition or call that would return in %st(0) (i686: float,
double, long double; x86-64: long double) is refused, since lccc does not
implement the GPR return convention. Unused statics are not diagnosed.

21 end-to-end flag/source combinations match GCC's accept/refuse pattern.
Tests: CLI last-wins matrix, x87 scanner vocabulary, IR boundary check.
