Subject: [PATCH 31/66] vectorize: exact `sar` for the clamped power-of-two
 vector trip count

The byte-IV reduction bound divides the signed element limit by the vector
width and then clamps the quotient to >= 0 before any use. Under that clamp
flooring equals truncating division: identical for limit >= 0, and for
limit < 0 both quotients are <= 0 and clamp to 0. One arithmetic shift
therefore replaces `cqto; idivq` (the backend has no power-of-two division
strength reduction for a division created this late, and its divrem pair
fusion makes a backend-side rewrite the riskier place for it).

The unclamped signed path keeps SDiv: there a floored negative bound would
start the scalar remainder below the loop's start.

Effect: every dynamic-bound byte-IV preheader loses `cqto; idivq` (20-90
cycles of latency on the loop-entry path, asm-verified on the I32 bound: `sarl $2`,
no `idiv`). It runs once per loop entry, so it matters for short trip counts;
the steady-state Ir gains measured with this series come from the guard-splat
hoist, not from this commit.

Tests: the bound builders assert `AShr` (no SDiv) for the clamped forms;
the upstream i128 oracle evaluator learns `AShr` as a floor shift, so its
comparison against the i128 reference proves the clamp absorbs the
floor/trunc difference on every sampled negative limit.
