Subject: [PATCH 37/66] x86: implement 256-bit integer PTEST
 (_mm256_test{z,c,nzc}_si256)

#972 completed the VTEST family but `_mm256_testz_si256`,
`_mm256_testc_si256`, `_mm256_testnzc_si256` and their GCC builtins
`__builtin_ia32_ptest{z,c,nzc}256` were missing: user code got an implicit
declaration and a PLT call to a nonexistent symbol.

`AvxTest` now carries an element kind (Ps/Pd/Int) instead of `double`, so
the payload-bit VPTEST can never be confused with the sign-bit VTEST (the
invariant upstream documents in `lower_avx_test`). Like GCC, the forms are
AVX (VEX.256 66 0F38 17), not AVX2; the raw builtins take v4di (either
element signedness). The 128-bit `_mm_test*_si128` stay SSE4.1 in
<smmintrin.h>. lcccsimd.h is regenerated from scripts/gen_lcccsimd.py and
the arity table gains exactly the three public names.

Tests: avx_ptest256_payload_bits{,_i686}.c probe each of the 256 bit
positions alone, against the other lane and its neighbour, plus 4096
random pairs, through wrappers and raw builtins (output equals GCC 16.2 at
-m64/-m32; UBSan clean). The feature gate checks AVX-not-AVX2 gating and
the v4di shape diagnostic on both ABIs; the header contract test pins the
wrappers.

The three names are added to the #982 public-API inventory
(engineering/isa/sse-avx-public-api.json, refreshed with --write) so
audit_sse_avx_public_api.py --check stays green.
