From 60af62362552a91266c5701359ca7b6d982d14d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:59:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 38/66] x86/i686: sound epilogue vzeroupper tracking on both
 ABIs

i686 never emitted an automatic vzeroupper: every function using 256-bit
AVX returned with dirty upper YMM state, so the caller's legacy-SSE code
paid the AVX-SSE transition (or, on newer cores, a false dependency on the
upper halves). GCC 16.2 cleans before `ret` on i386 as on x86-64. The
shared `dirty_upper_ymm` is now armed at the two i686 choke points every
256-bit operation passes through (`emit_avx_load` into a ymm,
`emit_avx_store_dest`) and at the YMM/ZMM argument/parameter moves; the
epilogue emits vzeroupper, except on the ymm0/zmm0 vector-return path
(it would destroy the returned value).

x86-64: the 64-byte YMM memcpy and the fixed-distance reduction reset
`dirty_upper_ymm = false` after their own vzeroupper. The flag is tracked
in emission order, not per path, so dirt left by an earlier-emitted block
on a path that bypasses the sequence was dropped and that path returned
dirty. The sequences now leave the flag as they found it.

Gate: check_intrinsic_feature_gate.sh requires a vzeroupper before every
`ret` of 256-bit VTEST/PTEST functions at -m64/-m32 and none for the
128-bit form.
