From 0a36f425bf86d0688a02d295191fbd79ad6289d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:11:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 42/66] x86: per-function target() narrowing is honoured end to
 end

defects, each reproduced on the s144 binary and checked against
GCC 16.2 (-march=haswell TU):

1. `arch=` added instead of replacing. `target("arch=x86-64")` kept the
   TU's AVX2 (lccc: vmovdqu/vpaddd %ymm; GCC: SSE2 paddd), BMI2 and
   AVX-512. Such a function is typically the baseline path of a runtime
   dispatch, so this was a SIGILL on the CPUs it exists for.
   `X86Isa::arch_level` now replaces the vector ISA (unmodelled CPUs get
   the x86-64 baseline, a subset of all of them), `ScalarIsa` resets
   before the CPU's grants, and the TU's AVX-512 bits are dropped under
   `arch=`. GCC keeps explicit command-line -m grants on top; they are
   not tracked apart from -march and are dropped too (fail-closed).
   The `ScalarIsa` doc that called the TU's extensions "never unsafe" to
   keep is corrected.

2. The whole-file peephole ran under the TU's ISA. `generate_function`
   restores the TU ISA after each function, so MOVBE fusion and SSE
   store forwarding rewrote narrowed functions with instructions they
   forbid: `target("no-movbe")` came out as `movbel` (GCC: bswap). The
   codegen now tracks the meet of the TU's and every emitted function's
   ISA and publishes it for the module peephole only; during emission
   each function's own ISA stays current (MachInst picks VEX vs legacy
   encodings from it). `isa::CURRENT` stays process-global on purpose:
   the metric-pair worker thread reads it, and a thread-local would
   break the byte-identical serial/parallel contract.

3. FP under a function's own denial was emitted silently.
   `target("general-regs-only")` compiled `double f(double)` to `addsd`
   and long double to x87; GCC refuses both ("SSE/x87 register return
   with SSE/x87 disabled"). The final-text fence that already enforces
   -mno-sse/-mgeneral-regs-only now also scans each function whose own
   attribute denies SSE (`general-regs-only`, `no-sse`), SSE2 (`no-sse2`;
   SSE1 `mulss` stays legal as in GCC) or x87 (`general-regs-only`,
   `no-80387`), on x86-64 and on i686 (which ignores per-function targets
   in codegen). Diagnostics name the function, the unit, the attribute
   entry and the offending instruction. The x86-64 `no-sse2` double is
   refused where GCC uses x87, exactly like module -mno-sse2.

Also: AVX512BW/DQ/CD imply AVX512F (GCC 16.2 compiles a
`target("avx512bw")` loop to zmm); `sse4.2` implying POPCNT (#979)
matches GCC 16.2 (`__POPCNT__` under -msse4.2).

Tests: arch replacement and meet (isa.rs), the scalar reset
(target_feature_isa_tests), per-function fence attribution
(sse_reference_tests), and check_target_attribute_isa.sh sections 5-6
(narrowing incl. the peephole, refusals on both ABIs with GCC
cross-checks). The extended gate fails on the pre-fix binary.
