From 239623ad2f3518196f04b98b8cc4c03055824242 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:19:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 43/66] trapv: i686 `long long` multiply no longer traps on
 every input

`-ftrapv` (#979) checks a signed 64-bit multiply through an exact I128
product. I128 is not an arithmetic type on ILP32 targets (GCC has no
`__int128` there, and the i686 backend carries only its low 64 bits), so
on i686 the check fired for every operand pair: `0 * 0` aborted.
The overflow builtins hit the same trap earlier and use a 64-bit-pair
lowering on these targets (torture pr91450, pr93494); the trapv multiply
now takes that path under the same `target_is_32bit()` predicate. x86-64
keeps the I128 form, where I128 is native.

Differential matrix against GCC 16.2 (14 signed/unsigned ops, 22x22
boundary operands, fork per case): x86-64 matched before and after;
i686 trapped 1221 of 6776 cases vs GCC's 910, all 311 extras being
`long long` products that fit.

check_driver_option_contract.sh only checked that `INT_MAX + 1`
aborts. It now runs the matrix on -m64 and -m32 against a reference TU
built without -ftrapv (division and unsigned wrap only, no shared code
with either overflow lowering), requires SIGABRT for every trap and
GCC's total of 910, and reports the first mismatches. GCC 16.2 passes
the matrix; the pre-fix compiler fails it at -m32.
