From 24a261669d0125e4cd4174cc5918b909dca773b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:30:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 44/66] target_clones: dispatch in GCC 16.2's priority order

When several clones are eligible on a CPU, the resolver tests them in
descending priority. #979's table disagreed with GCC's
`enum feature_priority` (i386-cpuinfo.h) in ways that select a
different clone than GCC on common hardware:

- `fma` ranked above `bmi2` (GCC: P_FMA < P_BMI2), so a Haswell picked
  the fma clone of target_clones("default","fma","bmi2");
- `popcnt` ranked below `sse4.1` (GCC: P_POPCNT > P_SSE4_2) and `bmi`
  below `avx` (GCC: P_BMI > P_AVX);
- `lzcnt`, `movbe`, `cmpxchg16b`, `f16c` and the AVX-512 sub-features
  ranked 420-850 although GCC gives them P_NONE;
- `arch=` names used one rank per level, where GCC distinguishes the
  CPUs (P_PROC_SSE4_2 nehalem/silvermont/goldmont/tremont, P_PCLMUL
  westmere, P_PROC_AVX, P_PROC_AVX2, P_PROC_AVX512F) from the
  `x86-64-vN` levels just above them, and ranks graniterapids at
  P_PROC_AVX10_1 above x86-64-v4.

The table now uses GCC's enumerator ordinals by name: features from
`isa_names_table` (i386-isas.h), CPUs from `processor_alias_table`
(i386-common.cc), with P_PROC_DYNAMIC and the levels resolved as
`get_builtin_code_for_version` (i386-builtins.cc) does. Ties keep the
user's order.

Reviewed and kept: lccc dispatches a named CPU on its feature set where
GCC uses `__builtin_cpu_is`. Each `arch=` clone is compiled with a
subset of what its resolver probes (`X86Isa::arch_level`), so this is
safe and lets newer CPUs of the same level run the clone. The probe
runtime gates VEX on OSXSAVE plus XCR0 & 0x6 and AVX-512 on XCR0 & 0xE6,
as libgcc does.

Runtime check (gdb, breakpoint on every `which*` symbol, on a CPU with
fma/bmi2/popcnt/sse4.2/movbe/lzcnt, so every clone is eligible): of the
five clone sets GCC 16.2 accepts, the previous table ran a different
clone than GCC in four (fma for bmi2, sse4_2 for popcnt, avx for bmi,
arch=haswell for arch=x86-64-v3); now all five match. GCC rejects
P_NONE features in target_clones ("ISA 'lzcnt' is not supported in
'target' attribute"); lccc keeps accepting them as an extension, since
each such clone is guarded by its own CPUID probe, and ranks them last.

Test: clone_priorities_follow_gcc_dispatch_order pins the three
inverted pairs, the P_NONE class and a 19-step chain through levels and
CPUs.
