From 3c89db9f9bc39d49277698eeddac73ef68651f6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:47:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 45/66] x86/i686: vzeroupper before ordinary calls with dirty
 upper YMM state

A function that wrote YMM state cleaned it only at `ret` and at sibcall
`jmp`s, so every ordinary call entered its callee with dirty upper halves.
On Skylake and later P-cores (including the Raptor Lake target) each
legacy-SSE XMM write in the callee then carries a merge and a false
dependency on the upper half; Haswell/Broadwell pay a ~70-cycle state
transition each way. GCC 16.2 and Clang 23.1.4 emit `vzeroupper` before
the call on both ABIs, e.g. for an AVX2 store followed by `sink(p)`.

Both call emitters now clean before the call when `dirty_upper_ymm` is
set. The exception is a call passing a 256/512-bit vector in %ymm/%zmm
(`CallArgClass::StructXmmReg` wider than 16 bytes), whose upper lanes
vzeroupper would destroy. The generic `emit_call` publishes that fact as
`call_passes_wide_vector`, the same test the sibcall exit applies through
`preserve_upper_vector_lanes`.

i686 overrides `emit_call`, so the trait default never runs there. The
first build of this change miscompiled the -m32 wide-argument case as
`vmovups (%esi), %ymm0; vzeroupper; call take`, passing a zeroed upper
half. The i686 override now sets the flag itself: conservatively from the
aggregate sizes on entry (the fastcall path keeps that value), then exactly
from `classify_call_args` (`StructXmmReg` wider than 16 bytes).

Soundness: SysV keeps no vector value in a register across a call. Every
XMM/YMM register is caller-saved, the allocator refuses call-spanning
vector homes (`spans_any_call`), IPA-RA models GPRs only, and the call
emitter already flushes deferred vector stores first. So a vzeroupper
right before the call can only clear dead state, apart from the
argument case above. `vex_promote` treats vzeroupper as a full overwrite,
so it can only gain promotions.

The flag stays set after the call. It is tracked in emission order, so
clearing it would let a path that bypasses the call return dirty (the
defect fixed in the epilogue commit); a redundant epilogue vzeroupper is
the cheaper error.

Gate: check_intrinsic_feature_gate.sh compiles a 256-bit PTEST before a
non-tail call (vzeroupper required before it) and a by-value YMM argument
(vzeroupper forbidden before the call) at -m64 and -m32, plus an i686
fastcall YMM argument. The awk matches the ABI call by name: an i686 PIC
function starts with `call __x86.get_pc_thunk.bx`, which had satisfied a
bare `call` pattern and hidden the -m32 miscompile above. The checks pass
on GCC 16.2's output and fail on both the pre-change compiler and the first
build of this change.
