From 5b8be96bab3971830bd473b13107a7a1d6072e6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:56:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 46/66] i686: pass native vectors to fastcall functions in SSE
 registers like GCC

GCC 16.2 passes `__m128`/`__m256`-shaped arguments of a fastcall function
exactly as cdecl does: in %xmm/%ymm0-2 in order, with the 4th and later on
the stack in argument order. Vector arguments never consume or break the
ECX/EDX chain; `function_arg_advance_32` advances only the SSE counters
for vector modes. Measured at -O2 -m32 -mavx2:

  t1(v2 a, int b)                        b=%ecx, a=%xmm0
  t2(int, v2 x, y, z, w, int, int)       %ecx, %xmm0-2, w at 0(%esp),
                                         %edx, last int at 16(%esp)
  callee(int a, v4 v, int b)             a=%ecx, v=%ymm0, b=%edx, `ret`

lccc's fastcall layout knew only ECX/EDX and stack slots, so it put every
vector on the stack and treated it as a chain break. The callee was worse,
because it disagreed with itself. `classify_params` gives the parameter
`F128SseReg`, so the prologue stored %ymm0, but the layout still reserved
the vector's stack bytes. `callee` therefore read `b` from 96(%esp) and
returned with `ret $36`. lccc-to-lccc calls read a garbage %ymm0, and a
GCC caller had 36 bytes popped from under it. The new gate leg fails on
the previous compiler in all three pairings: GCC to lccc and lccc to GCC
return 2, lccc to lccc returns 3.

The vector rule is now one function, `call_abi::i386_vector_reg_bytes`,
used by the cdecl/regparm classifier and the fastcall layout alike, so the
two conventions cannot drift apart again. The layout gains
`I686FastcallSlot::Vector { reg_idx, size }`.

Caller: vector registers are loaded after the stack writes (the by-value
copier uses %xmm0 as scratch) and before ECX/EDX (the address is staged
through %eax). Dynamic outgoing-area realignment considers stack-resident
arguments only, so a register vector no longer forces a 32-byte ESP
realignment. A >16-byte vector in %ymm/%zmm suppresses the pre-call
vzeroupper through the conservative `call_passes_wide_vector` value.

Callee: `ret $N` now counts only the remaining stack bytes. The capture
was already right (the `F128SseReg` arm). ParamRef treats a Vector slot
like a register parameter that has no stack home.

Variadic and -mno-sse calls are unchanged: the shared predicate requires
`vector_xmm_regs > 0`, and variadic calls keep vectors on the stack, as
cdecl does.

Tests:
* Unit test `fastcall_native_vectors_use_sse_registers_like_gcc`: the
  three GCC layouts above plus the variadic case.
* check_i686_gnu_vector_abi.sh runs GCC/lccc driver x callee pairs (not
  GCC x GCC) at -O0 and -O2. The prototypes weigh every argument
  differently, and the driver checks a stack guard after 64 calls. GCC x
  GCC passes the same program.
* check_intrinsic_feature_gate.sh's fastcall section now requires the
  %ymm0 load before `call takef`.
