From 8568fba6b423531a461a9e0c9604fccf583eabde Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:47:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 50/66] x86: route remaining SSE1 scalar XMM moves through the
 bridge

#980 made x86-64 -mno-sse2 keep scalar float legal on SSE1 and added
memory bridges (emit_scalar_gpr_to_xmm / emit_scalar_xmm_to_gpr) for the
float bit carriers. Several emitters still produced raw SSE2 moves on
paths that only exist once values have register homes, so lccc refused
valid float C at -O2/-O3 (360 of 480 differential runs on the rebased
tree) while -O0 was clean:

- operand_to_rcx (the float select's GPR cmov path), the call result
  moves to the accumulator, two value_to_reg routes, the float_ops and
  i128 libcall result paths: MOVD/MOVQ XMM<->GPR now go through the
  bridges.
- emit_copy_value: a mixed GPR<->XMM copy (a bit-cast float homed in a
  GPR feeding an XMM home) uses the bridge at the copy's width.
- emit_copy_value: the XMM-destination load width came from the
  constant kind only, so every F32 Value source was read as F64 -- an
  8-byte MOVSD over-read of its 4-byte slot, or a MOVAPD register copy.
  It now follows the copy's type (MOVSS / MOVAPS; also one byte shorter
  and no over-read under SSE2).
- The call-argument hazard spill uses MOVLPS (the SSE1 8-byte form of
  MOVSD) when SSE2 is off; SSE2 output is unchanged.

The SSE1 whitelist of check_legacy_sse_permissions.py lacked the SSE1
float->int conversions (CVTSS2SI/CVTTSS2SI and their l/q spellings), so
any legal probe converting float to int would have failed. The gate,
which runs from check_intrinsic_feature_gate.sh, gains probes for the
float select, float->int, the phi-copy reload, the GPR->XMM copy and the
__int128 libcall; each fails on the rebased tree before this change
(the float->int probe pins the whitelist).

Differential (scripts/fuzz_float_scalar_vs_gcc.py --mode sse1, 240 seeds
x -O0/-O2/-O2 -fomit-frame-pointer/-O3, UB-screened): 960/960
bit-identical to gcc-16 -O2 -mno-sse2, zero refusals; every -O2/-O3
assembly passes the gate's independent SSE1 whitelist.
