From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:52:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 52/66] x86: legacy-SSE FP-compare selects use a CMPSS/CMPSD
 mask, not a GPR cmov

Without AVX, try_emit_fp_select_blend refused every FP-compare select and
the caller materialised a ucomisd boolean and selected through a GPR
cmov: three XMM<->GPR crossings per select, the slowest form on every
x86 core. That is the whole SSE2 baseline (-O2 without -march).

Legacy SSE now emits LLVM X86 LowerSELECT's no-SSE4.1 shape: a cmpss/
cmpsd mask and a bit select, dest = ((T ^ F) & mask) ^ F, built in the
destination home (a +0.0 arm is a single andps/andnps). Legacy cmpsd only
has imm 0-7, so > and >= swap operands into LT/LE; EQ/NEQ/LT/LE give
the same unordered results as the VEX OQ predicates and the ucomisd
seta/setae fallback (LT/LE are the signaling forms, as C Annex F wants
for relational operators; values are identical). F32 stays SSE1-only, so
x86-64 -mno-sse2 float code also gets it.

llvm-mca 23.1.4 (alderlake / znver4), glibc floor select chain: 40.0 ->
29.0 / 28.0 -> 26.0 cycles per iteration. libm_round_family -O2 hot loop:
25.09 -> 20.02 / 32.73 -> 24.82 cycles per iteration. Callgrind Ir rises
5.9% on that program (rename-eliminated movapd copies count as
instructions); it is still 15% below GCC 16.2.

tests/regression/fp_select_sse_mask.c crosses every predicate with the
arm shapes taking distinct paths (generic, +0.0 either side, -0.0, which
must not take the zero shortcut, computed, swapped, call-spanning result
without an XMM home) over +-0, +-inf, NaN payloads and denormals, hashed
bitwise; GCC 16.2 output at -O0..-O3, FPO and -mavx2. Wrong predicate,
swapped arms or a broken zero shortcut each change the hash (checked by
mutating the emitted assembly). check_legacy_sse_permissions.py's SSE1
whitelist gains cmpss/cmpps and their pseudo-op spellings (the compiler's
fence already allowed them) plus two select probes.
