From 21df00dc5c9709d8d0f80c6f9a547c0f96e6d412 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:36:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 58/66] x86: _mm_stream_ps is MOVNTPS; SSE1 memory intrinsics
 gated at SSE1

Four permission and lowering defects in the legacy-SSE intrinsics, found
while extending the SSE1 permission test (#980/#982's gate):

* `_mm_stream_ps` was a plain store (`*(__m128 *)p = a`). That dropped
  the non-temporal hint GCC and Clang keep. At -m64 it cost a register
  copy and a push. At `-m32 -mno-sse` it compiled silently into GPR
  copies, where GCC rejects it. It is now `__builtin_ia32_movntps` /
  `IntrinsicOp::Movntps` next to Movntdq/Movntpd in every pass that
  classifies non-temporal stores (store target, alignment 16, full-result
  overwrite, copy coalescing). It is SSE1-gated. Both backends feed it a
  MOVUPS load: MOVDQU/MOVUPD are SSE2 and MOVUPS is the float-domain
  load. -m64 output is now `movntps %xmm0, (%rdi); ret`, as with GCC.
* `_mm_load{,u}_ps` / `_mm_store{,u}_ps` shared the SSE2 Loaddqu/Storedqu
  builtin identities, so `-mno-sse2 -msse` refused every SSE1 program
  that touched memory (GCC accepts). They get their own SSE1-gated
  identities (`X86LoadPs`/`X86StorePs`) on the same IR ops. The i686
  backend now picks MOVUPS when SSE2 is off; x86-64's `xmm_move` already
  did.
* `_mm_sfence` was ungated. GCC 16.2 rejects it under -mno-sse, and pre-SSE
  i686 silicon raises #UD on it. It is now SSE-gated.
* `_mm_clflush` was ungated. GCC gates it on SSE2; a Pentium III has SSE1
  but no CLFLUSH. It is now SSE2-gated.

A census mapped every public SSE..SSE4.2 name that resolves to a builtin
and compared it against its header's ISA level. It found no other
mismatch: `_mm_pause` is ungated in GCC too, and CRC32 is SSE4.2 as in
GCC.

check_legacy_sse_permissions.py (1106 -> 1262 legs, both ABIs):
* its SSE1 vocabulary now matches the driver's allowlist (rcp*, rsqrt*,
  movntps, cvt*ps*pi);
* it probes `rcp`/`rsqrt`, the four load/store names, sfence (refused
  without SSE, legal on SSE1-only), clflush (refused without SSE2), and
  both spellings of the stream store. For the stream store it also
  requires MOVNTPS in lccc's output.
Runtime: `simd_movnt_sse1.c` and its -m32 sibling (values, -0.0, untouched
neighbours, a streamed loop) match GCC 16.2. The s162 binary rejects both.

Type facts: `__builtin_ia32_movntps` is added to
engineering/isa/gnu-x86-builtin-types.tsv verbatim from GCC 16.2
i386-builtin.def (sha256 8eaa8802..., OPTION_MASK_ISA_SSE,
VOID_FTYPE_PFLOAT_V4SF). The generator learns the `PFLOAT` pointer
token, and x86_builtin_types.rs plus gnu_native_builtin_types.c are
regenerated, never hand-edited. This keeps the
generated-gnu-builtin-types-sync gate green.

Its public-API inventory entry (sse-avx-public-api.json) now records the
`__builtin_ia32_movntps` raw builtin and the canonical override
(--write).
