From 48916fc0c431bbbac28dd204b938bc1be4dcc81d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:24:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 59/66] x86: _mm_stream_pi is SSE1-gated MOVNTI, never a silent
 plain store

`_mm_stream_pi` was a C struct copy (`*p = a`) with no permission check:
* it compiled under -mno-sse, where GCC 16.2 refuses it ("target specific
  option mismatch");
* it dropped the non-temporal hint even where SSE2 MOVNTI exists.

It is now `__builtin_ia32_movntq` / `X86Movntq`. The type facts come
verbatim from GCC 16.2 i386-builtin.def: OPTION_MASK_ISA_SSE,
VOID_FTYPE_PULONGLONG_ULONGLONG. Direct calls are lowered at the call
site, so a function's own target("sse2") counts. The permission is SSE1,
as in GCC. The instruction choice:

* SSE2 usable in the function: `Movnti64`. That is one `movnti %r64` on
  x86-64, the code GCC emits there (`movq %xmm0,%rax; movnti`) minus the
  cross-file move, because lccc's __m64 already lives in a GPR. On i686
  it is two `movnti %r32`, the existing pair lowering and Clang 23.1.4's
  code.
* SSE1 only: a plain 8-byte store. MOVNTI is SSE2. The only SSE1
  non-temporal 8-byte store is MOVNTQ, which needs an MMX register. MMX
  registers alias the x87 stack and leave the tag word full until an EMMS
  that lccc's x87 code never issues, so using MMX here would corrupt later
  long-double/x87 arithmetic. The plain store keeps the value exactly and
  loses only the cache hint, on Pentium III/Athlon-XP-class targets.
  Clang 23.1.4 and ICX 2026.2 drop the hint even at -m64 (`movsd`).

`x86_feature_permitted` is factored out of the permission check. It is
the same TU-effective-ISA plus function target(...) computation, now
reusable for instruction selection.

The generator learns the PULONGLONG/ULONGLONG tokens; the generated
contract (x86_builtin_types.rs, gnu_native_builtin_types.c) is
regenerated, not hand-edited.

Tests:
* simd_stream_pi{,_m32,_sse1}: x86-64, i686+SSE2, i686 SSE1-only. They
  cover ten bit patterns (sign/half boundaries), both spellings,
  constants, a streamed loop and untouched neighbours, and match GCC
  16.2's hash (identical across -O0/-O2, -m64/-m32, SSE1/SSE2). The
  previous binary rejects the raw builtin.
* check_legacy_sse_permissions.py, both ABIs: refused without SSE;
  accepted SSE1-only with SSE1-legal output and no movnti or %mm; MOVNTI
  required with -msse2; a target("sse2") caller in an SSE1-only TU gets
  MOVNTI. The new `forbid=` probe option makes "no MMX, no SSE2 on
  SSE1-only" an executable contract.
* Unit test: both spellings resolve to the SSE requirement.

Its public-API inventory entry records `__builtin_ia32_movntq` and the
canonical override (--write).
