From 4020c1a36ac029e83614f24817601d12c349444f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:41:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 60/66] x86: CVTPS2PI and i686 64-bit CVTSS2SI/CVTSD2SI round
 per MXCSR

A new differential test drives all 90 __m64 entry points through lane
boundaries and xorshift inputs against GCC 16.2's real-MMX code (GCC and
Clang 23.1.4 agree on all 72 hashes). It found three pre-existing
miscompiles, all the same defect: `_mm_cvtps_pi32`, `_mm_cvtps_pi16` and
`_mm_cvtps_pi8` used a C cast, which truncates. CVTPS2PI rounds per
MXCSR.RC, round-to-nearest-even by default, so 1.5 -> 2, -7.5 -> -8 and
32767.5 -> 32768 (saturating to 0x7fff for pi16). lccc returned 1, -7
and 32767. Each lane is now `_mm_cvtss_si32` (CVTSS2SI, SSE1, MXCSR
rounding). For pi16/pi8 the result is then saturated, which equals GCC's
PACKSS composition, and NaN/overflow give the integer indefinite, which
saturates to 0x8000/0x80 as in GCC.

`_mm_cvtss_si64` / `_mm_cvtsd_si64` on i686 (no 64-bit CVT*2SI exists
there; GCC and Clang offer the names only on x86-64) fell back to a C
cast. That is the cvtt* contract, not this one. The emulation is now
exact under all four MXCSR.RC modes:
* |x| < 2^23 (float) or 2^52 (double): one ADDSS/SUBSS (ADDSD/SUBSD)
  pair with copysign(2^k, x). The sum's ulp is exactly 1, so the
  hardware rounds once, per MXCSR, and the cast is then exact.
* Up to 2^63 the value is already integral, so the cast is exact.
* NaN and |x| >= 2^63 give the integer indefinite, as the instruction
  does.
The stale "TODO: _mm_cvtss_si32 truncates" comment is removed:
lcccsimd.h's `_mm_cvtss_si32` has been CVTSS2SI for a while.

Tests:
* mmx_intrinsics_differential{,_m32}: all lccc __m64 APIs, 72 hashes vs
  GCC. They match at -O0/-O2, -m64/-m32, SSE1-only and -march=x86-64-v3.
  The old headers fail 3 hashes.
* sse_cvt_si64_rounding{,_i686}: 35 floats and 36 doubles (ties,
  2^23/2^31/2^52/2^63 edges, NaN, inf, denormals) in all four rounding
  modes, plus the cvtt* forms, against tables recorded from the x86-64
  hardware instructions. The i686 sibling is lccc-only (.env) because
  GCC has no such API there. The old headers fail it.
* The #982 public-API inventory records the new wrapper dependencies
  (--write).
