From 6b496456ab594e7fe9adb663666bf9271dec3696 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:27:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 62/66] x86: SSE1-only XMM transfers of 8-byte SSE-class values

x86-64 passes and returns 8-byte vectors (`float x2`, `int x2`, and
`__m64` once it is GCC's vector type) and the SSE-class eightbytes of
small structs in XMM registers. Under `-mno-sse2 -msse` there is no
MOVQ/MOVSD between an XMM register and a GPR or memory, but six paths
used them unconditionally, so lccc's SSE2 guard rejected the function:

* the parameter capture store (`emit_capture_store_xmm`);
* the single-eightbyte call-result store (`store_xmm_to`, MOVSD);
* the two-eightbyte call-result stores (INTEGER+SSE, SSE+INTEGER,
  SSE+SSE);
* the callee's eightbyte return (`load_bits64_to_xmm0`);
* the XMM-home argument copy (MOVAPD);
* the struct eightbyte argument load
  (`emit_small_aggregate_eightbyte_load`).

Without SSE2 they now use MOVLPS for memory (the same eight bytes; the
upper lanes are not part of an eightbyte), MOVAPS for register copies,
and the existing balanced stack bridge for GPR crossings, which is what
GCC 16.2 emits. With SSE2 the output is unchanged.

`check_legacy_sse_permissions.py` requires
`void f(__m64 *p, __m64 a) { _mm_stream_pi(p, a); }` to compile with
`-m64 -mno-sse2 -msse`; the next commit makes `__m64` a vector, so
without this one the gate fails. `vec8_abi_sse1_only{,_o0}.c` pass
`float x2`, `int x2`, `{F2, long}`, `{long, F2}` and `{F2, F2}` as
arguments and results against GCC (the previous compiler rejects both
legs); the next commit adds the `__m64` shapes.
